module v2.lens.decl_consumption

import std.types { Bool, Int, String }
import std.algebra { Empty, FreeMonoid }
import v2.std.algebra {
  HeadAbsent,
  HeadFound,
  length,
  list_head,
  list_snoc_item,
}
import v2.std.optional { Optional, Present, Absent }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, decl_ref }
import v2.std.node { Node, NodeFold, Symbol, Atom, TypeNode, fold_node }
import v2.lens.common.construction_justification {
  ConstructionJustification,
  ConstructionClass,
  WallAfterGrounding,
}
import std.disposition { SingleAuthority }
import v2.std.decl_index { DeclFact }
import std.realization_schedule { ScheduleWitnessEntry }
import v2.std.decl_ref_resolution {
  DeclarationIdentity,
  declaration_identity_from_fact,
  declaration_ref_target_qualified_name,
}
import v2.std.compilers.lexing { symbol_lexeme }

data decl_consumption_authority_note: String = "THE DECLARATION-GRAIN CONSUMPTION AUTHORITY. It answers one question -- which declarations reference this declaration -- and it is the authority BOTH the documentary standing gate and the PR delivery check consume, so that neither has to approximate it and neither forks it. Before this module the repository had module-to-module reachability (v2.lens.module_graph dependency_closure), doc-to-doc reachability (v2.lens.doc_reachability), and per-reference citation resolution (v2.std.decl_ref_resolution), and none of them was this: v2.lens.witness_cost_locality witness_cost_locality_precision_frontier states in tree that module grain IS the declared precision frontier and that fn-grain reachability does not exist. That note dissolves here.\n\nWHY MODULE GRAIN HAD TO GO RATHER THAN BE TIGHTENED. It is FAIL-OPEN in exactly the case that matters to both consumers: a declaration added to a module that already has dependents reads as reached, because the MODULE is reached, while nothing references the declaration at all. Add a realization row naming it and the standing reads Realized; add a scaffold and the purpose law reads it consumed. Both answers are wrong in the direction that lets dead work look alive."

data decl_consumption_universe_note: String = "RESOLUTION IS OVER THE DECLARATION UNIVERSE (decl_facts), NOT THE BINDING UNIVERSE (SymbolIndex), AND THE TWO ARE NOT THE SAME SET. v2.std.symbol_index already carries global_bare: Map<Symbol, GlobalBareBindingState> whose GlobalBareUnique/GlobalBareAmbiguous pair is precisely the uniqueness question asked below, so reusing it was the first thing considered and it is the right instinct. It is the wrong authority here for two reasons, stated so this is a considered decision rather than an unnoticed fork.\n\nFIRST, DIFFERENT UNIVERSE: SymbolIndex is filled from the containment tree by v2.compiler.symbol_index_fill and contains every BINDING -- parameters, let binders, fields -- while the question here is which DECLARATIONS exist and whether a name picks exactly one of them. A parameter named the same as a module-scope declaration makes the binding universe ambiguous while the declaration universe stays unique, and answering the declaration question from the binding index would refuse edges that are perfectly determined. SECOND, DIFFERENT COST: filling a SymbolIndex requires parsed module roots for the corpus, while decl_facts is host-backed and is already the enumeration authority every other declaration-grain lens consumes.\n\nRECONCILIATION TRIGGER, named rather than left implicit: when the namespace lane makes the containment tree the single naming authority, declaration identity and binding identity become two walk-rules over ONE index, at which point this module consumes that index's declaration projection and the map built below deletes. Until then these are two questions over two populations, and the shape coinciding is not the same as the authority coinciding."

data consumer_identity_note: String = "A CONSUMER IS CARRIED AS ITS DeclarationIdentity, NOT AS A DeclarationRef ASSEMBLED BY STRING SURGERY. The dispatched shape named DeclarationRef on both sides, and the composer that exists in tree runs the other way: v2.std.decl_ref_resolution declaration_ref_target_qualified_name turns module_path + decl_name INTO a qualified name, and nothing decomposes one. Splitting on the final dot to manufacture the missing direction would be a second naming scheme for something the containment tree already names, decided by punctuation -- the §3 class, and it would fabricate a module_path for any qualified name whose shape the guess did not fit.\n\nSO THE INDEX IS KEYED ON QUALIFIED NAMES, which is what decl_facts natively carries, and the point query takes a DeclarationRef and COMPOSES it with the existing authority. The consumer side then carries DeclarationIdentity -- qualified_name, name, kind, rel_path together -- which is strictly more than the ref would have carried and is produced by declaration_identity_from_fact rather than parsed out of a string."

type DeclarationConsumer
  = DeclarationReference { consumer: DeclarationIdentity }
  | ExecutionRoot { root: DeclarationIdentity }
  | WitnessRoot { witness: DeclarationIdentity }
  | GeneratedProjectionRoot { artifact: String }
  | ExternalContractRoot { contract: DeclarationIdentity }

data consumption_unresolvable_note: String = "AN EDGE THAT CANNOT BE DECIDED IS CARRIED, NEVER DROPPED, and the reason is the failure mode this whole program is about. A dropped undecidable edge renders as 'this declaration has no consumers', which is the empty-observation narrow -- and the action a reader takes on that answer is to DELETE the declaration or retire the document that describes it. A widen would merely be expensive; this narrow is destructive. So every candidate that resolves to more than one declaration, or to a name the universe does not declare as a subject at all, lands in a typed row that the standing fold must dispose of."

type ConsumptionUnresolvable
  = SubjectNameAmbiguous {
      name: String,
      consumer: DeclarationIdentity,
      candidate_count: Int,
    }
  | SubjectUniverseAbsent { subject_qualified_name: String }

type DeclarationConsumption {
  subject: DeclarationRef
  consumers: List<DeclarationConsumer>
  unresolvable: List<ConsumptionUnresolvable>
}

data decl_consumption_precision_frontier: String = "THE RESIDUAL IMPRECISION, MEASURED AGAINST ITS DIRECTION RATHER THAN ASSERTED ABSENT. An edge here means: the consumer declaration's own Node subtree contains an atom whose identity resolves to exactly one declaration in the universe, and that declaration is the subject. That is a real syntactic occurrence of the subject's name inside the consumer, which is why it is a sound improvement on module grain -- module grain reports an edge when the name appears NOWHERE in the consumer.\n\nWHAT IT STILL OVER-APPROXIMATES: a local binder inside the consumer sharing a name with a module-scope declaration produces an edge that no real reference justifies. The direction is fail-open, so it is named rather than buried. It is narrow -- it requires a name collision between a local binder and a declaration, and the corpus census reports 98% of declaration names globally unique -- but narrow is not zero and this row exists so nobody reads the index as exact.\n\nDISSOLVE-ON: binder-aware scope, which is the SAME containment-tree walk the reconciliation trigger above names -- lexical lookup up the ancestor chain decides whether an atom occurrence is bound locally or reaches a declaration, and at that point the occurrence is classified rather than assumed. One structure closes both residues, which is the argument for waiting for it instead of hand-rolling a binder scan here."

fn decl_consumption_atom_names(root: Node) -> List<String> {
  fold_node(
    n: root,
    algebra: NodeFold {
      init: fn(self) {
        match self.kind {
          TypeNode { connective } =>
            match connective {
              Atom { identity } => [symbol_lexeme(sym: identity)]
              _ => []
            }
          _ => []
        }
      },
      step: fn(acc, _edge, sub) { concat(acc, sub) }
    }
  )
}

data subject_name_index_note: String = "THE NAME INDEX IS BUILT ONCE PER RUN, WHICH IS WHAT MAKES THE POINT QUERY AFFORDABLE. The measurement that killed the previous design is on record: a corpus-wide reverse query at 409s wall, ~92% of it in load_bare_edge_index, which no per-PR consumer could pay per subject. The shape that fixes it is not a cheaper traversal, it is amortization -- one pass over the declaration universe builds name -> declaration, one pass over each declaration's subtree collects its atoms, and every subsequent subject question is a lookup. A consumer that rebuilds the index per subject has reintroduced the cost this module exists to remove."

type SubjectNameBinding
  = SubjectNameUnique { qualified_name: String, rel_path: String }
  | SubjectNameAmbiguousBinding { count: Int }

fn decl_consumption_name_bindings(facts: List<DeclFact>) -> Map<String, SubjectNameBinding> {
  fold(facts, init: empty_map(), f: fn(acc, f) {
    match v2.std.collection.map_get(acc, f.name) {
      Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => acc
      Accepted { value: opt, diagnostics: _ } =>
        match opt {
          Absent =>
            map_insert(
              m: acc,
              key: f.name,
              value: SubjectNameUnique {
                qualified_name: f.qualified_name,
                rel_path: f.rel_path,
              },
            )
          Present { value: SubjectNameAmbiguousBinding { count } } =>
            map_insert(
              m: acc,
              key: f.name,
              value: SubjectNameAmbiguousBinding { count: count + 1 },
            )
          Present { value: SubjectNameUnique { qualified_name: existing, rel_path: _ } } =>
            if existing == f.qualified_name {
              acc
            } else {
              map_insert(
                m: acc,
                key: f.name,
                value: SubjectNameAmbiguousBinding { count: 2 },
              )
            }
        }
    }
  })
}

type OutboundEdge {
  subject_qualified_name: String
  consumer: DeclarationIdentity
}

type OutboundScan {
  edges: List<OutboundEdge>
  unresolvable: List<ConsumptionUnresolvable>
}

fn decl_consumption_outbound_for_fact(
  fact: DeclFact,
  bindings: Map<String, SubjectNameBinding>,
) -> OutboundScan {
  let consumer = declaration_identity_from_fact(fact: fact)
  fold(
    decl_consumption_atom_names(root: fact.node),
    init: OutboundScan { edges: [], unresolvable: [] },
    f: fn(acc, atom_name) {
      match v2.std.collection.map_get(bindings, atom_name) {
        Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => acc
        Accepted { value: opt, diagnostics: _ } =>
          match opt {
            Absent => acc
            Present { value: SubjectNameAmbiguousBinding { count } } =>
              OutboundScan {
                edges: acc.edges,
                unresolvable: list_snoc_item(
                  xs: acc.unresolvable,
                  item: SubjectNameAmbiguous {
                    name: atom_name,
                    consumer: consumer,
                    candidate_count: count,
                  },
                ),
              }
            Present { value: SubjectNameUnique { qualified_name: target, rel_path: _ } } =>
              if target == fact.qualified_name {
                acc
              } else {
                OutboundScan {
                  edges: list_snoc_item(
                    xs: acc.edges,
                    item: OutboundEdge {
                      subject_qualified_name: target,
                      consumer: consumer,
                    },
                  ),
                  unresolvable: acc.unresolvable,
                }
              }
          }
      }
    }
  )
}

data self_reference_exclusion_note: String = "A DECLARATION DOES NOT CONSUME ITSELF, and excluding the self edge is load-bearing rather than tidy. Every fn in this dialect names itself at its own definition site, and several are host-backed stubs whose entire body is a call to themselves, so counting the self occurrence would give EVERY declaration in the corpus at least one consumer and the purpose law would report a universally green corpus by construction -- the rung-inflation shape, where the mechanism cannot fail. The exclusion is by qualified name, so a genuine recursive call is also not a consumer of itself, which is the same fact and the correct answer to it."

type DeclarationConsumptionIndex {
  by_subject: Map<String, List<DeclarationConsumer>>
  unresolvable: List<ConsumptionUnresolvable>
  subject_universe: Map<String, Bool>
}

fn decl_consumption_absorb_scan(
  index: DeclarationConsumptionIndex,
  scan: OutboundScan,
) -> DeclarationConsumptionIndex {
  let with_edges = fold(scan.edges, init: index, f: fn(acc, e) {
    match v2.std.collection.map_get(acc.by_subject, e.subject_qualified_name) {
      Rejected { diagnostics: _ } => acc
      Accepted { value: opt, diagnostics: _ } => {
        let existing = match opt {
          Absent => []
          Present { value: xs } => xs
        }
        DeclarationConsumptionIndex {
          by_subject: map_insert(
            m: acc.by_subject,
            key: e.subject_qualified_name,
            value: list_snoc_item(
              xs: existing,
              item: DeclarationReference { consumer: e.consumer },
            ),
          ),
          unresolvable: acc.unresolvable,
          subject_universe: acc.subject_universe,
        }
      }
    }
  })
  DeclarationConsumptionIndex {
    by_subject: with_edges.by_subject,
    unresolvable: concat(with_edges.unresolvable, scan.unresolvable),
    subject_universe: with_edges.subject_universe,
  }
}

fn build_declaration_consumption_index(
  facts: List<DeclFact>,
) -> DeclarationConsumptionIndex {
  let bindings = decl_consumption_name_bindings(facts: facts)
  let universe = fold(facts, init: empty_map(), f: fn(acc, f) {
    map_insert(m: acc, key: f.qualified_name, value: true)
  })
  fold(
    facts,
    init: DeclarationConsumptionIndex {
      by_subject: empty_map(),
      unresolvable: [],
      subject_universe: universe,
    },
    f: fn(acc, f) {
      decl_consumption_absorb_scan(
        index: acc,
        scan: decl_consumption_outbound_for_fact(fact: f, bindings: bindings),
      )
    }
  )
}

data point_query_refusal_note: String = "A SUBJECT THE UNIVERSE DOES NOT DECLARE IS REFUSED, NOT ANSWERED WITH ZERO CONSUMERS, and this arm is the whole reason the index carries subject_universe. Without it the two states collapse: 'this declaration exists and nothing references it' and 'this declaration is not in the fact pack at all' would both read as an empty consumer list, and a consumer acting on that answer deletes the declaration or retires the document in both cases. The second state is routinely a universe hole rather than a fact about consumption -- the corpus does not whole-tree-resolve, so a subject outside the assembled pool is expected -- which makes the collapse the empty-observation narrow aimed directly at live code."

fn declaration_consumers(
  index: DeclarationConsumptionIndex,
  subject: DeclarationRef,
) -> DeclarationConsumption {
  let qn = declaration_ref_target_qualified_name(ref: subject)
  match v2.std.collection.map_get(index.subject_universe, qn) {
    Rejected { diagnostics: _ } =>
      DeclarationConsumption {
        subject: subject,
        consumers: [],
        unresolvable: [SubjectUniverseAbsent { subject_qualified_name: qn }],
      }
    Accepted { value: present, diagnostics: _ } =>
      match present {
        Absent =>
          DeclarationConsumption {
            subject: subject,
            consumers: [],
            unresolvable: [SubjectUniverseAbsent { subject_qualified_name: qn }],
          }
        Present { value: _ } =>
          match v2.std.collection.map_get(index.by_subject, qn) {
            Rejected { diagnostics: _ } =>
              DeclarationConsumption {
                subject: subject,
                consumers: [],
                unresolvable: [SubjectUniverseAbsent { subject_qualified_name: qn }],
              }
            Accepted { value: opt, diagnostics: _ } =>
              DeclarationConsumption {
                subject: subject,
                consumers: match opt {
                  Absent => []
                  Present { value: xs } => xs
                },
                unresolvable: [],
              }
          }
      }
  }
}

data construction_justification_note: String = "WHY THIS IS A READER AND NOT YET A WALL, classified rather than assumed. The bad state -- an authored declaration that no live consumer reaches, and a documentary claim that outlives the structure grounding it -- is DECIDABLE given the declaration universe, so §5 forbids calling it a ratchet: the word never is the trap, and there is a real path to unwritability here rather than an undecidable residue.\n\nWHAT BLOCKS THE WALL TODAY IS THAT CONSUMPTION IS NOT A LOCAL PROPERTY. A declaration and its consumer can be authored in one commit, so nothing at the authoring site distinguishes the good case from the bad one; only a whole-corpus read decides it, and a whole-corpus read is a reader. That is why this module is honestly a lens even though the class it serves is decidable.\n\nDISSOLVES TO SingleAuthority: when the containment tree becomes the single naming authority, a reference is an edge in one structure the compiler already walks, and an unreached declaration is refusable at compile time the way an unbound name already is -- at which point the bad state has no Accepted program containing it and this reader is replaced rather than supplemented. That is the same trigger the universe note and the precision frontier name, which is the argument for one grounding rather than three local repairs."

data construction_justification: ConstructionJustification = ConstructionJustification {
  class: WallAfterGrounding { dissolves_to: SingleAuthority }
}

data root_enumeration_note: String = "A ROOT IS A DECLARATION NOTHING NEEDS TO REFERENCE, AND THE UNIVERSE OF ROOTS IS ONLY PARTLY ENUMERABLE HERE. That is a finding rather than an inconvenience, and it is stated before the mechanism so no reader takes the roster below for the whole set.\n\nWHAT IS ENUMERABLE: an explicit roster of ScheduleWitnessEntry carries entry (a source path) and function (a declaration name), so joining it to decl_facts by (rel_path, name) yields real declarations. That join is legitimate rather than positional citation in disguise -- the roster natively carries a path and a name, and this join CONSUMES the identity the roster already has instead of inventing one for a declaration that has a better name available.\n\nWHAT IS NOT: since the discovery flip, most executing witnesses are found by a host-side directory scan (CiSpec.discovery_scan_dirs), which no .dag fold can enumerate -- the scan happens outside the substrate and only its results reach the runner. Generated-artifact producers are likewise dispatched by a match in gunbc.generated_artifact_emit rather than listed as a roster of declaration names. So a purpose law consuming this module MUST treat an empty root set as unknown rather than as proof that a declaration is unrooted, which is why the unavailability is a typed arm and not an empty list.\n\nTHE SHAPE OF THE FIX, named rather than attempted here: discovery becomes a declared population the substrate can read, which is the same fact the witness-admission ruling already demands for a different reason -- a witness that no cadence executes must not read as covered. One authority discharges both."

type RootEnumeration
  = RootsEnumerated { roots: List<DeclarationConsumer> }
  | RootEnumerationPartial {
      roots: List<DeclarationConsumer>,
      unenumerated_source: String,
    }

fn decl_consumption_roster_roots(
  roster: List<ScheduleWitnessEntry>,
  facts: List<DeclFact>,
) -> List<DeclarationConsumer> {
  fold(roster, init: [], f: fn(acc, entry) {
    fold(facts, init: acc, f: fn(inner, f) {
      if f.rel_path == entry.entry && f.name == entry.function {
        list_snoc_item(
          xs: inner,
          item: WitnessRoot { witness: declaration_identity_from_fact(fact: f) },
        )
      } else {
        inner
      }
    })
  })
}

data root_enumeration_is_always_partial_note: String = "THE ARM IS FIXED AT PARTIAL, DELIBERATELY, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF TYPING IT. There is no argument available today by which this function could return RootsEnumerated, because the host-side discovery scan is not readable from here -- so offering the total arm and never selecting it would be a carrier shaped to flatter a future it cannot yet reach. The arm exists because the population becomes enumerable once discovery is declared, and on that day this function selects it and the string below deletes. Until then every consumer matches an arm that says out loud that the set is incomplete."

fn decl_consumption_roots(
  roster: List<ScheduleWitnessEntry>,
  facts: List<DeclFact>,
) -> RootEnumeration {
  RootEnumerationPartial {
    roots: decl_consumption_roster_roots(roster: roster, facts: facts),
    unenumerated_source: "host-side witness discovery (CiSpec.discovery_scan_dirs) and generated-artifact producer dispatch (gunbc.generated_artifact_emit) are not readable as declaration rosters from .dag",
  }
}
